  "rationale": "The CLI does use `clap`, but the requirement is not marked completely, and nothing links a test to it.\n\n**What is correct**\n- `src/main.rs:10` marks `#[derive(Parser)] struct Cli` with `{impl}`requirements.cli.clap``.\n- `src/main.rs:90` marks the `Cli::parse()` call in `main` with the same `{impl}`.\n- `Cli` holds a `#[command(subcommand)] command: Command`, and `main` dispatches on the parsed result.\n- I found no hand-rolled argument parsing.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Incomplete `{impl}` marking.**\n   - The requirement says clap must \"define and parse its commands and options.\"\n   - The definitions live in `enum Command` (`src/main.rs:18-76`), `enum MdbookCommand` (`src/main.rs:79`) and `enum HookCommand` (`src/main.rs:84`).\n   - Those enums carry the `#[derive(Subcommand)]` attributes, `#[arg]` options, `ArgGroup` constraints, the `author-evidence` alias and the hidden `hook` command.\n   - None of them has an `{impl}` reference to `requirements.cli.clap`, and `references` reports only two sites. The code that does most of the \"define\" work is therefore unmarked.\n2. **No `{test}` reference to the requirement.**\n   - `references` shows no `{test}` or `{cite}` sites for it.\n   - Tests such as `tests/cli.rs` `unknown_command_fails_with_usage` and the `--help` checks in `tests/commands.rs:705,764` do exercise clap-generated usage and help output.\n   - They are tagged only to other requirements, such as `requirements.cli.invocation`, so the evidence does not tie any test to this requirement.",
